There is a moment before judgment when a thing is allowed to be itself. It is usually a very short moment.

Then we reach for a comparison. What it should have been, what someone else would have done, what we would have done—under circumstances we have quietly improved for ourselves.

Standards are useful. They help us notice the difference between what is and what might be. But they can also become a way of not looking. Once something has been classified, we feel that we have understood it.

{% marginNote %}

A standard can clarify what we see. It can also decide, in advance, what we are able to notice.

{% /marginNote %}

## The distance between seeing and knowing

I wonder how much of what we call discernment is simply the speed with which we recognise something familiar. The category arrives before the encounter has finished.

Perhaps the question is not whether to judge, but whether we have stayed with a thing long enough to know what we are judging.

A little more attention will not make every judgment kinder. It might, however, make it more accurate.
